{{also|bonafide|bonâ fide}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2007|April|20}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|bonâ fide}}
* {{l|en|bona fyde}} {{q|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From the {{bor|en|la|[[bonā]] [[fidē]]|t=in good faith}}, the {{glossary|ablative case}} of {{m|la|[[bona]] [[fidēs]]|t=good faith}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˌbəʊ.nəˈfaɪ.di/|nocount=1}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈboʊnə.faɪd/|/ˌboʊnəˈfaɪdi/|/ˈbɑnə.faɪd/|/ˈboʊnəˌfiːdeɪ/|nocount=1}}
* {{a|Canada}} {{IPA|en|/ˈboʊnə.faɪd/|nocount=1}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-bona-fide 2.ogg|Audio (US)}}, {{audio|en|en-us-bona-fide.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-bona fide.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-bona fide.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

====Usage notes====
The pronunciation {{IPAchar|/ˈboʊnə.faɪd/}}, is the most common one in the USA and therefore listed first in American dictionaries, incl. American Heritage, Merriam-Webster, Webster's Third New International Dictionary, and the American version of Collins.<ref>Webster's Third New International Dictionary, Unabridged</ref>

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-|head=bona fide}}

# In good faith; [[genuinely]], [[sincerely]].
#: {{syn|en|bonafidely<q:nonstandard>}}
#* {{RQ:Sterne Tristram Shandy|chapter=X|volume=III|pages=29–30|pageref=30|passage=But by the knots I am speaking of, may it please your reverences to believe, that I mean good, honest, devilish tight, hard knots, made '''''bona fide''''', as ''Obadiah'' made his; {{...}}}}
#* '''1791''', {{w|Joseph Priestley}}, ''Letters to Burke'', XII:
#*: Let thinking people, then, judge what must be the fate of a church, whose fundamental doctrines are disbelieved by men of sense and inquiry, whose articles are well known not to be subscribed '''''bonâ fide''''' by those who officiate in it {{...}}.

